  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continue to differ on the \nmagnitude and positioning of the subtropical ridge early next week\nwhich will dictate how hot temperatures get and how soon rain \nchances will return to our area. Both the EPS and GEFS show the \nridge strengthening over the Southern Plains through the middle of\nthe week which will allow hghts aloft to increase across much of \nthe Desert Southwest. In turn, temperatures will also increase \nfrom near normal Monday to slightly above normal on Tuesday. The \ntemperature forecast becomes much more unclear heading into the \nmiddle of next week with large spreads in the NBM interquartile \nrange. This is likely due to differences in the magnitude of the \nridge with many EPS members showing a stronger ridge with 500 mb \nhghts approaching 596-598 dam while the GEFS shows slightly lower \noverall hghts. For now NBM temperatures show a cooling trend after\nTuesday which is likely due to an increase in moisture as \nsoutheasterly flow aloft returns to the lower deserts. \n\nPWATs are expected to increase back to around 1.5-1.8\" by the \nmiddle of next week which will bring rain chances back into the \npicture by the Wednesday-Thursday timeframe when PoPs increase to \naround 20-40% areawide. The GFS is even showing an easterly \nwave/inverted trough arriving into southern AZ along with \nadditional tropical Pacific moisture on Wednesday which could \nboost rain chances significantly, but we will likely have to wait \nfor more run to run consistency as these features are always \ndifficult to resolve at longer time scales. Either way, most of \nthe GEFS and EPS members are showing measurable precipitation \nacross portions of the area by the middle of next week.",
